<p>President Donald Trump met Vietnam’s top leader, Communist Party chief To Lam, at the White House on Friday for their first formal in-person meeting. Trump said he would work to remove Vietnam from U.S. lists that restrict access to advanced American technology. The talks came after the announcement of deals valued at more than $30 billion.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/1u1fHy3hGK3ZctuTGa3kl4-QV6DZNouX6YiXX8n2CH4zx4uBmv9zU8_UR1qM6_PP0ofmN3x_dTKDythPDiE-MbmZdPzW5k3hWUnnwP7qz8syQCsPhj8yu8oF6sgaNvvxifWhp5wUptPGH6XsiYvIxF9jtOzVnPgXv5_bb2BXecySRAD-xF3uLIz_8g' alt='Vietnam’s top leader To Lam, who met President Trump at the White House.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Agency follow-through: Vietnam’s government said Trump would “instruct the relevant agencies” to remove Vietnam from a restricted technology-access list — a reminder that any change would depend on U.S. administrative steps.</li><li>Sequence of events: The White House meeting followed Lam’s attendance at the inaugural session of Trump’s “Board of Peace” in Washington.</li><li>Provenance of the readout: The pledge surfaced through a Vietnamese-government summary posted on Vietnam’s government news website, not through a detailed U.S. account.</li><li>Hanoi’s framing: Vietnam’s government cast the pledge as a move toward closer economic and technology cooperation.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Vietnamese government: Vietnam’s government said Trump would instruct relevant U.S. agencies to soon remove Vietnam from a tech export-control list that restricts access to advanced U.S. technologies, framing it as a concrete deliverable from the White House meeting. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/southeast-asia/article/3344194/us-remove-vietnam-tech-export-control-list-hanoi-says'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>The Straits Times framing: The Straits Times noted that the meeting came after the announcement of deals worth more than $30 billion, underscoring the business backdrop 